issues.suggestion:
Consider adding explicit type checking or using nullish coalescing: 'weights ?? {}'. This makes it clear you're only handling null/undefined, not other falsy values like 0 or false. Example:

const {
  fuzzy: fuzzyWeight = defaultSearchOptions.weights.fuzzy,
  prefix: prefixWeight = defaultSearchOptions.weights.prefix
} = weights ?? {}

Consider creating branded types or using template literal types for better type safety:

type Weight = number & { readonly __brand: 'Weight' };

function createWeight(value: number): Weight {
  if (value < 0 || value > 1) {
    throw new Error('Weight must be between 0 and 1');
  }
  return value as Weight;
}

Or add runtime validation in the merge logic:

const fuzzyWeight = weights?.fuzzy ?? defaultSearchOptions.weights.fuzzy;
if (fuzzyWeight < 0 || fuzzyWeight > 1) {
  throw new Error('fuzzy weight must be between 0 and 1');
}

Define a base type and use Partial:

interface WeightsConfig {
  /**
   * @default 0.45
   */
  fuzzy: number
  /**
   * @default 0.375
   */
  prefix: number
}

interface SearchOptions {
  weights?: Partial<WeightsConfig>
  // ... other options
}

This makes the type more maintainable and clearer about the relationship between required and optional versions.

Ensure this change is properly documented as a minor version bump (new feature: partial weights support) rather than a patch. Add migration notes for users who may need to update their TypeScript strict null checks. Consider adding runtime validation to maintain the same guarantees as before:

if (weights && (weights.fuzzy !== undefined || weights.prefix !== undefined)) {
  if (weights.fuzzy !== undefined && typeof weights.fuzzy !== 'number') {
    throw new TypeError('weights.fuzzy must be a number');
  }
  if (weights.prefix !== undefined && typeof weights.prefix !== 'number') {
    throw new TypeError('weights.prefix must be a number');
  }
}

